## Browser sensor engine provenance and notices

The browser sensor page is an experimental, direct-to-device feature. Its
release artifact is intentionally a stripped opaque WebAssembly binary; this
public repository does not publish the engine's protocol source, private
vectors, device traces, credentials, certificate material, source maps, or
private source locations. WebAssembly raises reverse-engineering cost but is
not a secrecy boundary.

The engine's behavioral reference provenance is [MIT-licensed
LoopKit/G7SensorKit](https://github.com/LoopKit/G7SensorKit) and
[LoopKit/CGMBLEKit](https://github.com/LoopKit/CGMBLEKit). The implementation is
an independent reimplementation; those projects' names do not imply affiliation,
endorsement, or clinical validation. No GPL-licensed xDrip source is included in
this repository or in the binary.

The production WASM dependency graph is locked in the build that produced the
artifact. Direct runtime dependencies are `aes` 0.8.4, `p256` 0.13.2, `sha2`
0.10.9, and `zeroize` 1.9.0, each available under MIT and/or Apache-2.0. The
binary also contains the following transitive runtime crates under their
permissive notices: `base16ct`, `block-buffer`, `cfg-if`, `cipher`, `const-oid`,
`cpufeatures`, `crypto-bigint`, `crypto-common`, `der`, `digest`, `ecdsa`,
`elliptic-curve`, `ff`, `group`, `hmac`, `inout`, `primeorder`, `rand_core`,
`rfc6979`, `sec1`, `signature`, `typenum`, and `version_check` (plus
`zeroize`'s support crates) under MIT OR Apache-2.0 or the equivalent ordering
used by each
upstream package). `generic-array` is MIT; `subtle` is BSD-3-Clause. Build-only
macro crates in the lock graph are not embedded in the WASM bytes. The
artifact's manifest records the build receipt and high-level license family;
the upstream package metadata is the source of the exact notice terms.

The importer performs Bluetooth reads, history normalization, analysis, and
CSV/NDJSON exports locally in the browser. Nothing from the connected sensor is
uploaded. An optional remembered credential is encrypted with WebCrypto before
local IndexedDB persistence when available; remembering it is off by default,
and the page offers a forget action. Browser memory, developer tools, extensions,
crash reporting, or a malicious script running on the same origin can still
expose local data, so this feature is not a security boundary.

The importer does not monitor in the background, does not guarantee complete
history, and is not medical advice or a medical device. Do not use its output
for diagnosis, treatment, or dosing decisions.
